===== sruman --help
Steam Linux Runtime manager: install and update runtimes, run native games in them through profiles and prefixes

Usage: sruman [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  remove       Remove runtimes
  info         Show a runtime: build, channel, sizes, dependencies, users
  runtimes     List installed runtimes
  files        List an installed build's files
  verify       Check installed builds against their file lists (all without arguments)
  import       Install from local archives or directories
  run          Run a game from games.toml
  execute      Run an executable once with a profile
  shell        Open a shell in a profile's runtime and prefix
  status       Show running games and sessions
  stop         Stop games, profiles' sessions or processes
  killall      Stop everything sruman started
  list         List games
  show         Show a game: executable, profile, runtime, prefix, environment
  check        Check games and profiles without launching (all without arguments)
  logs         Print a game's latest run log
  init         Create or update a profile's prefix
  profiles     Manage profiles
  steam        Run Steam on sruman's runtimes: replace Steam's own copies with links to the same builds
  provide-umu  Make a runtime umu's folder under `$XDG_DATA_HOME/sruman/umu/`, so umu runs it instead of downloading its own
  help         Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -y, --yes
          Answer every prompt with its default

      --color <WHEN>
          Colorize the output
          
          [possible values: auto, always, never]

  -q, --quiet
          Less output (`runtimes`: names only)

  -v, --verbose...
          Log to stderr (-vv: more)

      --config <FILE>
          Use this config.toml

  -h, --help
          Print help

  -V, --version
          Print version

===== sruman remove --help
Remove runtimes

Usage: sruman remove [OPTIONS] <RUNTIMES>...

Arguments:
  <RUNTIMES>...
          

Options:
      --force
          Remove even if a profile uses it (never a build in use)

